WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.007 --> 00:00:03.090
MYLES WARD: Hi, everybody.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.860
My name is Myles Ward.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.189
I'm the global head of solutions
for Google's Cloud platform.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.830
And I'm super excited
to present to you today

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.420
about a topic that's very
near and dear to my heart.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.780
I've been operating
application infrastructure

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.810
for over a decade
today, built a startup

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.460
and operated its systems.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.610
And I've worked through
hundreds of businesses

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.100
as they translate
their infrastructure

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.920
and their operations from
a private environment

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.410
or from other facilities
into public cloud.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.910
So what I'm hoping
to do is walk you

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.190
through in a relatively
informal way.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.680
I'm going to talk as plainly
as I can about what I've

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.020
seen work, what I've
seen be functional,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.090
what kinds of gotchas
there are as you

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.330
come onto Google Cloud, what
sorts of things to expect,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.850
so that we can create
an opportunity for you

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.970
to be able to capture as
much value as possible

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.222
from our infrastructure.

00:00:52.222 --> 00:00:54.180
And to start with that
I'm going to need to ask

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.410
the developers to get out.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.960
Anybody a developer in the room?

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:58.830
Hardcore developers?

00:00:58.830 --> 00:00:59.405
Good.

00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:00.780
They're all in
the other meetings

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.950
talking about
rewriting everything.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.874
Leave them that
part of the work.

00:01:06.874 --> 00:01:08.040
I like software development.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.690
I think it's an
interesting challenge.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.770
It's nowhere near as fun
as keeping things alive,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.900
delivering value over time.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.030
Building the first
time is frankly kind

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.070
of the sort of fun part, right?

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.810
This is the real
working part where

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.830
we're building a system that
can survive the test of users,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.860
the test of data,
and the test of time.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.760
So I expect that for
many of you this new move

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.610
to cloud, the concept of public
cloud as an infrastructure

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.290
creates a bunch of
new opportunity.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.623
And I can tell you that the
future is very bright there,

00:01:39.623 --> 00:01:42.030
and it also potentially
presents some challenges.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.670
And I want to talk
through those challenges

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.040
and make sure that we're
being as clear as we can

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.520
around what you can expect
as you come to public cloud.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.750
So as I understand it-- and as
I'm talking to customers every

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.420
day--

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.270
what I see in
practice is a system

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.940
that's born out of the
history of technology

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.530
in practice at every
business, everywhere.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.730
So maybe you rent a chunk
of a collocation facility,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.070
or you've got a
data center or two.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.310
You have redundancy and
availability provided

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.650
by more than one virtual
machine or maybe more than one

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:14.890
physical host--

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.580
but not 3s or 10s or 20s.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.240
You have applications that
are installed often manually.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.770
Sometimes you've
got some automation

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.510
that's been built
in there that's

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.670
managing the way that
applications get deployed,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.800
either in virtual environments
or in bare metal environments.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.550
And then you've also got a bunch
of weird stuff tucked around

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.302
under the corners.

00:02:33.302 --> 00:02:35.010
You got a desktop
under the water cooler.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.180
You got a machine that sits
underneath Billy's back

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.550
build box that's where all
the really fun stuff happens.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.490
Like the complexity
of these systems

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:48.060
is born out of their
creative construction

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.830
as they're built over time.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.590
You probably have at
least one or two Hadoops.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:53.690
I'll tell you a funny bit.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.990
My start up, we experimented
with Hadoop when it was 0.0001.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:01.110
And we went to a conference
and we were calling it Heydoop

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.480
for like three months,
because we had no idea how

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.771
it is that you talk about that.

00:03:04.771 --> 00:03:05.790
It's a clear item.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.410
Listen for anybody who
mispronounces the names of very

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.050
complicated technical products.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:15.270
It means they learned
about it by reading.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.780
You want those
people on your team.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.240
They read the docs.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.490
So we also are seeing
lots of businesses

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.190
use a whole panoply of
different storage systems.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.290
I think some of the
highest complexity

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:36.330
sits in filers and databases
of a zillion varieties

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.540
and the application connectivity
between those databases.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.250
That can be some
of the stuff that's

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.850
some of the most complicated
in non-dynamic public cloud

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.560
environments.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.310
And I think most of the assembly
of these kinds of systems,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.880
again, is happening at
the same pace as business.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.760
You have a quarterly
set of goals.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.074
You have a relatively
short planning window.

00:03:58.074 --> 00:04:00.240
You have a view to where
it is that your business is

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.830
trying to go for
a specific product

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.200
launch or a specific
regional launch,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.500
or just trying to be able
to do the next update

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.024
of physical infrastructure.

00:04:09.024 --> 00:04:10.440
And that means
that because you're

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.200
making this sort of relatively
short planning horizon

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.050
you take locally
optimal choices.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.089
You say, well, if we
need one more box,

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:19.511
let's buy one more box.

00:04:19.511 --> 00:04:21.510
Instead of taking a minute
to step back and look

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.860
at the overall systems and be
able to evaluate for what's

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.610
most efficient in writ large.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.730
Our environment-- we hear
from customers quite a lot--

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.220
is that there's this
very big opportunity

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.310
in the middle of a movement,
in the middle of a transition

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.290
from one kind of
technology to another

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.500
to clean up some of the
bad choices of the past.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.380
We see people try to use it
as an investment in reduction

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.324
in technical data
or operations data.

00:04:48.324 --> 00:04:49.740
Try to take the
opportunity-- when

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.660
we do that cloud thing
we're going to do it right.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.493
We're going to get
serious about automation.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:56.350
We're going to get knee deep
into this whole new world

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.070
of stuff and make sure that
everything works properly.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:04.770
And that movement opportunity is
one that frankly flies totally

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.220
in the face of all of the ITIL
best practices that you've

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.710
been told that says, change
just one thing at a time,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.620
move all the stuff,
don't screw with it,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.120
make sure that it
works in that way,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.570
and then start removing
individual building blocks

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.920
and pieces.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.620
So we want to help
you think through

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.650
which are the kinds of places
where a little bit of change

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.670
as you move makes sense,
will reduce your risk,

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:31.852
will make it so that these
systems operate more reliably,

00:05:31.852 --> 00:05:33.810
and which changes maybe
you should hold off on.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.510
Make it so that you move
in a way that's easier

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.010
to digest and understand.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.310
So we hear all the time
that-- oh, man, oh, man.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.890
Once this sort of magical
mystical system in the Cloud

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.810
take over everything,
operations will

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.352
be so incredibly easy that we
will never have to do anything.

00:05:48.352 --> 00:05:50.143
The server list thing
just means that there

00:05:50.143 --> 00:05:51.520
are no operators in the world.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.070
I will give you a hint, and I
don't even have to make it up.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:54.570
It's great.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.970
I get to quote the
chairman of Google.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.880
And he says, "The number
one limiting resource

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.450
for businesses is our
technical skills."

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.170
Right?

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.910
He just said it onstage
like 20 minutes ago.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.870
You are all the technical
people that he's talking about.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.800
None of your businesses are in a
situation where all of a sudden

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.260
they find themselves with
this incredible opportunity

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.720
to get rid of their best,
brightest, and most talented

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.910
people in order
to save $1 or $2.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.250
In reality, most
companies are dying

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.600
to find more talented,
aggressive, innovative folks.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.400
And I can think of
no better way for you

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.830
to give evidence that you are
in that category of operators

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.470
than by taking on the
challenge of this more

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.680
complicated and more
technically challenging

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.910
move to public cloud.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.470
That can kind of sound like
sort of a crappy sales pitch,

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.650
but I also watch it happen.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.474
I go into companies, and
over the course of months

00:06:50.474 --> 00:06:52.390
a couple of their folks
stand up and say, I've

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:53.590
got to figure out
how this thing works.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:54.820
And somebody gets
up, I can see him

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:56.650
in the console clacking
away at things.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:58.025
And then a couple
of months later

00:06:58.025 --> 00:07:00.760
that person's performance
is seen as great.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:04.150
And I see new job titles on
business cards and everything

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.450
starts to move in a way that
I think most employees want.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.000
So I also want to make
sure that you recognize

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.020
we are on the hook with you.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.990
It's super easy to be able
to quote the chairman.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:18.430
It makes my job really nice.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:22.210
He listed this whole
litany of teams at Google

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:24.830
that are committed to
our customer's success.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:26.827
That's the engineers
on the Google side

00:07:26.827 --> 00:07:27.910
that are here to help you.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:29.868
And I'll restate the
list, because I think it's

00:07:29.868 --> 00:07:31.240
super useful to understand.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.910
We have at the very
beginning of your interaction

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.710
with us, customer engineers.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.530
Those are folks that
are dedicated resources

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:41.260
for working with our customers
to ensure that you understand

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.437
our platform, that you know what
building blocks are available,

00:07:44.437 --> 00:07:46.270
that you have access
to all of our resources

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.290
for on-boarding education.

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.380
And as you have clear
questions about how

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:53.440
our systems are built, they're
the first point of contact.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:58.690
As they want and you want a
little executive air cover.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:01.250
You have a boss or their
boss or somebody is saying,

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.250
I don't about this
public cloud thing.

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:04.540
That seems kind of--

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:06.410
we have folks for that.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.530
That's called the
Office of the CTO.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.200
So if you want to interact
with folks from us that

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:13.810
were CTOs or CIOs
at other businesses

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.019
that have been there and
done this transition before,

00:08:16.019 --> 00:08:17.643
we have full time
Google employees that

00:08:17.643 --> 00:08:18.910
will come help you with that.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.234
If both of those folks say
the problem is not the bosses,

00:08:22.234 --> 00:08:23.650
the problem is
that they're trying

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:26.070
to do something that's
particularly unique,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.230
and they have a pattern that
we haven't heard of before--

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.230
that's my team,
solutions architecture.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.669
We'll come help ensure
that what you're designing

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:34.960
is best practice.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:37.240
Ensuring that the
operations patterns

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:40.690
that you want to follow meet
the needs of your customers.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.700
And making sure that
that's something

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.400
that gets documented,
so that you can reuse

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:47.350
those patterns over and over.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:51.190
Everything that's at
cloud.google.com/solutions is

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.170
an output of that work effort.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.300
We've also got folks in
professional services that

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.520
can augment your
staff as you build up

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.800
for a big transition
or migration.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.415
We have folks in
customer engineering--

00:09:02.415 --> 00:09:04.540
it's called customer
reliability engineering-- that

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.310
will sit with you
years after you

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:09.580
launch to ensure that your
operations practices follow

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:14.440
the Google SRE model, follow the
model for high scale web facing

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.580
operations.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:19.171
So we are recommending
some changes,

00:09:19.171 --> 00:09:20.920
and those changes are
born out of the fact

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:24.410
that this model is
way more efficient.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:27.760
So I have seen now 30
different spreadsheets

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.940
from 30 different
companies just this year

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:34.510
describing to me what
their actual cost

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:36.211
of their private
environments are.

00:09:36.211 --> 00:09:37.210
I will give you a hint--

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:40.380
none of those have
the same line items.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.390
Nobody has the same list.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.980
There's not some
sort of central place

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.614
where all the actual
costs get written down.

00:09:46.614 --> 00:09:48.030
And most of the
time this stuff is

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.190
done by departments
where they inherit

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:53.220
some of the basic
infrastructure from other parts

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.390
of the business.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.810
That's very powerful if
what your business unit only

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.930
has to pay for is maybe the
new computers or the SAS

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:02.670
applications that
you want to deploy.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:07.050
But for what we're watching,
those externalities

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.620
can be very real constraints
in your ability to be nimble.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.570
So I work with companies all
the time that grow, grow, grow,

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:16.380
grow until they have
filled up that data center,

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.090
and now they need another one.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.820
And that next one
computer costs a whole lot

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.730
more than the last one.

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:25.830
And so that independence
from scale--

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:27.330
does not matter if
you want a couple

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.760
of pieces or a whole
bunch of pieces--

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.730
allows you to stop thinking
about the power and the cooling

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.460
and all of the rest of the
infrastructural building

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:35.970
blocks.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:39.570
How many folks have
screwed around for hours

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:43.080
with routing tables in the core
routing of your data center?

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:43.860
Folks done that?

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:44.730
Yes?

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:45.660
Sucks.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:47.530
Super sucky.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:48.060
Right?

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.610
And then you're in the middle
of trying to figure out,

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.080
how do I redo addressing?

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.038
And what's the number
range we're going to use?

00:10:54.038 --> 00:10:56.100
And, oh, does that overlap
with these other guys?

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.450
You delete all that stuff.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:02.730
We have a petabyte a second
bi-sectional bandwidth

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:04.260
already installed.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:08.850
At any point you need a little
more network it's already here.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.099
You want the network for
another 400 machines?

00:11:11.099 --> 00:11:11.640
That's great.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.598
It's already installed.

00:11:12.598 --> 00:11:14.760
So that all of the
work associated

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:18.240
with that instrumentation moves
up a layer of abstraction.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.220
And that's the change
that we're recommending.

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.882
Rather than you logging
into the individual switch

00:11:22.882 --> 00:11:24.840
and making sure that it's
configured in the way

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.870
that you want, or
altering the firewall

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.240
on this individual
firewall box, you

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.630
do that logically at
our service layers

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:35.890
to ensure that the same
changes that you apply

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:39.960
are systematically applied
across all of your projects,

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:42.060
across all of your applications.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.250
That will make sure that
you have the ability

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:45.630
to sort of multiply your effort.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.990
And we think about this
stuff in a lot of ways.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:51.930
When I've done operations,
I got really frustrated

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:53.610
that I wasn't Superman.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.870
That I couldn't stay up 24
hours a day, seven days a week.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.474
And that I wasn't able to
move at the speed of sound

00:11:59.474 --> 00:12:01.140
from where my desk
was over to the colo,

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.600
and then back to my desk,
and then back to the colo,

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:03.990
and back to the desk.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:07.890
I needed laser beams
to be able to adjust.

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:11.110
We think the public cloud gives
operators an incredible set

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:14.920
of new superpowers.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:19.760
My favorite would be a total
independence from procurement.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.930
How many folks have a
clear buy in from somebody

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.100
to buy the machine?

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.172
You've got a clear
need for the machine.

00:12:26.172 --> 00:12:27.880
You know exactly the
spec of the machine.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:29.740
You've got a vendor that's
going to sell you the machine.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:31.780
You got the form all
filled out for the machine.

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:33.160
And then it'll
totally be shipped

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.576
here in like three weeks, but
no it's actually four weeks.

00:12:35.576 --> 00:12:40.480
And no, no, they've lost it at
the dock, and it's seven weeks.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.870
All of that stuff
is just deleted

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:45.730
when you have a
provider that is buying

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:49.500
a fifth of the computers
that are manufactured.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.620
We have leftovers.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.330
I swearsies.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:56.710
So that extra
capacity allows you

00:12:56.710 --> 00:13:00.370
to react to your stakeholders
much more nimbly.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:03.490
They say, I need a build
box this afternoon.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.920
And you say, how about
36 seconds from now?

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:11.989
Because that's the TP 99
launch rate on VMs today.

00:13:11.989 --> 00:13:13.030
Don't even launch a form.

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:13.780
Forget that.

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:15.680
It's one rest call.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:17.290
So put a little wrapper on it.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:20.040
Put a snazzy, green
button on a web page.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.040
Hand it to him.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.380
Let them off to the
page, smash the button,

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:24.670
they have their own dem box.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:25.810
Get out of my hair.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.500
I have real work to do.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:31.500
That kind of change in the
operational model where

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.660
your partnership with a
couple of those developers

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.760
may be building up a
little bit of the tooling,

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.670
building up a little bit of
the access instrumentation,

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:44.250
can really shave off the
un-unique, un-differentiated

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.390
work of some of the
base operations.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:48.459
So again-- superpowers, right?

00:13:48.459 --> 00:13:50.250
Environment can get a
zillion times bigger.

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:51.666
And then when
you're done with it,

00:13:51.666 --> 00:13:54.920
it can go away and
go away perfectly.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:58.770
I spent so many hours just
cleaning up network cable

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.680
trash on the bottom of my cage.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:04.380
Just cleanup, just
deleting all of your stuff,

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.210
just throwing out
all the mess, getting

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.722
rid of the cardboard box and
getting rid of all the stuff.

00:14:08.722 --> 00:14:10.180
You don't have any
of that with us.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.360
You're able to just
shrink down to zero

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.600
by saying I don't like this
project anymore I'm closing it.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.394
Now it's gone.

00:14:16.394 --> 00:14:18.060
You want another one
back that same way,

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.070
you can use a deployment
manager template

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.080
to stand up that whole
infrastructure over again

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:22.830
from scratch.

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.780
You need three copies
of it this afternoon.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:25.960
Stab, stab, stab.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:31.250
Deployment manager will deploy
three copies of that rapidly.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:33.590
You also give yourself
some op superpowers

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:35.260
around your interaction
with developers.

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:38.480
So I take sort of
personal umbrage

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.160
at the concept of DevOps.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.860
I've worked at places that
were the inventors of the term,

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:46.370
and I've seen it morph and
become a different thing

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:48.200
every other afternoon.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:51.290
DevOps now gets just sort
of stuffed into the bucket

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:53.480
that operations is, and
I think that's unfair.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:55.412
There's a very separate
practice there.

00:14:55.412 --> 00:14:56.870
Google's written
quite a lot about.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.570
We call it on our side, SRE.

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:02.360
And a big part of
that practice is

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:06.470
being able to not only build
systems designed not to break,

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:10.370
but enable systems that fix
themselves when they're broken

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:11.870
because they're going to break.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:14.630
And so a lot of the work
around instrumenting

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:20.150
for easy rollback, easy deploy
and retract, AB testing,

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.360
subdivision of different patch
releases into the application,

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.030
all that stuff, there's quite a
lot of tooling on Google Cloud

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.190
to enable that.

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.742
So we expect that
you already have

00:15:29.742 --> 00:15:31.700
most of these building
blocks, that they're not

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:32.660
particularly new.

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:35.447
You probably have
virtual machines.

00:15:35.447 --> 00:15:37.780
You probably have some kind
of clustered storage system.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.650
You probably have
relational databases,

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:41.270
NoSQL databases of some kind.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:44.570
How many folks have
incredible nights

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:49.720
of fun staying up super late
maintaining those SQL clusters?

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:50.820
Yes, you love it.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:51.920
You have a great time.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:52.420
See?

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:55.340
There were very few hands
there and lots of tears

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:56.340
in the back of the room.

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.760
So those jobs are
particularly painful

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.380
because the
applications that you're

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:07.400
managing, while they hold the
crown jewels of your business,

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.160
in most cases, the
real core data that

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:11.710
powers your applications.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.331
Those applications
are new, very new.

00:16:14.331 --> 00:16:16.330
And they're built in the
open, which is awesome.

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.163
We are super huge
supporters of open source.

00:16:18.163 --> 00:16:20.660
We've given a lot of technology,
particularly for data

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:22.790
storage and data management
to the open source.

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:26.570
But when I talk to operators
what I hear from them

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.840
is I want something that
is absolutely war tested.

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:32.360
I want something that's run
hundreds of times bigger

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:33.020
than me.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:35.600
I want to know for
certain that it operates.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.230
So you heard Eric
describe Spanner.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:42.170
Spanner is the relational store
that sits behind every Google

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:42.740
service.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:46.460
It operates at hundreds
of millions of operations

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:49.070
a second in aggregate
across it's zillions

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:50.480
of back-end systems.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.000
And it is globally
transactionally consistent.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.400
It scales as wide
as you want to go.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:58.040
And you don't have to mess
with figuring out your sharding

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.870
structure, or figuring
out how you're

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:01.970
going to do
re-indexes under load,

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:04.579
or load balancing between
read and write levels.

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:06.599
All that stuff's deleted.

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:09.030
That kind of battle
tested infrastructure,

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:12.960
Spanner is a single example, but
is present across Google Cloud

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:14.790
platform's products.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:17.310
So Datastore is the
data store that--

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:18.810
Datastore is the data store--

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:20.730
Datastore is the
system that they

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:23.790
talked about during the
keynote about Pokemon Go.

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:27.599
That's where they're writing
50 times as many transactions

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:30.540
as other environments
totally unnotified.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.210
Anybody have 50 times
as many computers

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:36.480
as you currently own laying
around but not using them?

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:40.531
And just sort of
keeping them for fun?

00:17:40.531 --> 00:17:41.030
No?

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:41.157
OK.

00:17:41.157 --> 00:17:41.760
Cool.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.490
I was just double checking.

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:48.290
That availability
of skilled resources

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:50.010
is just very powerful.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:54.450
That said, we're hoping that
not everything requires change.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.870
So it's my experience that
a really substantial number

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.540
of the tools-- the
monitoring, metering,

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:04.500
management applications,
automatic deployment, CICD,

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:07.530
the kinds of applications that
you've built on top of public

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:08.400
data center--

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:11.280
those are already compatible.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:15.570
We've built and done a lot of
work in our development effort

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:20.100
to ensure that your systems of
record are already compatible

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:20.610
with us.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:25.140
We've done work with being
able to do monitoring systems

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:28.140
and visualization systems,
Graphite, Influx, Grafana,

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.372
all of these tools
plug-in to our services

00:18:31.372 --> 00:18:32.580
without any kind of big pain.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:34.412
We have some first
party stuff for this,

00:18:34.412 --> 00:18:36.120
and we think that they
are good products.

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:38.460
But Google's commitment
to open ensures

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.880
that we'll continue to make
our applications permeable,

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:44.580
transparent to the
kinds of monitoring

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:46.690
and metering that you
want to be able to apply.

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:49.290
And that means that a lot of
the run books and the sort

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:52.890
of basic approaches that you're
using to operations management

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.150
still work today.

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:58.380
And this partnering spans all
of the different components

00:18:58.380 --> 00:18:59.060
of the stack.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:02.640
We're really excited about
what that allows folks to do.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.680
So another example
of the stuff that's

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:08.430
really good-- how many folks
have used Datadog before?

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:09.500
I like it.

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:12.290
Stand up, turn around, wave.

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:13.590
He's from Datadog.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:14.940
It works really good.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:19.101
So those kinds of tools,
they work very powerfully

00:19:19.101 --> 00:19:20.100
in private environments.

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.141
But I think they also get
a couple of superpowers

00:19:22.141 --> 00:19:24.180
by being present
in public cloud.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:26.430
And our value
proposition to you is

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:29.970
to be able to use the same
panes of glass, the same views

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.250
that you're used to seeing
in your own environments

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:33.960
on our environment.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:38.070
And that adds a lot
of value from what

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:41.940
I've seen in reducing the
risk of that transition.

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:45.000
Making it so that it's just
more straightforward to see

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.800
a comparison of the two moving.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:50.310
And so that's like probably
the very first operations best

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:53.160
practice that I'd describe.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.020
Because our systems
are inexpensive,

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:56.700
because we give
them to you for free

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:58.350
for $300 for the
first several months,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:01.995
because we're in the middle of
making them cheaper every day,

00:20:01.995 --> 00:20:04.370
you really should be able to
take at least some component

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:06.830
of your application and have
it run on us in a way that

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:08.390
seems familiar to you.

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.640
Not just-- I can turn it
on, but turn it on and hang

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:14.510
the full spectrum of
your current management

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:18.170
and operations tooling
around that application.

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:20.890
I will tell you this
was my experience--

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:24.800
individual single computers are
very reliable building blocks

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.450
today.

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:28.880
They do things
like you'd expect.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:32.060
Any time you move past the
behavior of a piece of software

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:34.550
on a single computer, and you
have more than one computer,

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:37.610
all of a sudden those building
blocks become substantially

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.890
unreliable and crappy.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.490
So I'd be really interested--

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:44.780
and you can feel free to
reach out to me personally

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:47.010
after this talk or any
point in the future.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:48.980
This is a big part of
our commitment to being

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:50.870
available to our customers.

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:54.830
I'd be really interested to hear
as you do those tests what's

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:57.500
the reliability, the
consistency of bandwidth,

00:20:57.500 --> 00:20:59.690
the consistency of
network latency,

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:03.320
the consistency of access to
disk and disk performance,

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:06.650
the consistency of access
to remote storage systems,

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:09.930
in comparison to the private
environments that you deploy.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:13.220
Even environments that are
designed very purpose built,

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:14.900
very single tenant,
very monolithic

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:16.790
around an individual
application,

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:20.150
Google has so radically
over provisioned some

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:24.320
of these basic resources that
they can feel really, really

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:28.370
reliable, and really, really
consistent in comparison

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:31.940
to even your environments that
are private and dedicated,

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:34.619
and certainly in comparison
to other public cloud

00:21:34.619 --> 00:21:35.160
environments.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.550
That's at least what
we measure today.

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:39.470
So what are those steps?

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.580
How would you do that, right?

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:45.110
We see this happen
in two halves.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:49.690
So the first is finding
stuff that's a little nasty,

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:53.030
The Brownfield.

00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:54.770
Maybe you're not
really in the middle

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:58.640
of considering that the core,
beating heart of the business.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:01.970
It's the second and
third tier applications

00:22:01.970 --> 00:22:06.650
that are more work from
an operations standpoint

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:08.860
than they probably
deserve to be.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.369
Those kinds of applications you
can earn immediate time savings

00:22:12.369 --> 00:22:13.910
by having them run
in the environment

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:15.980
where your operations
overhead is lower.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:19.700
So they can be a very important
step in bringing applications

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.830
into a public environment.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:25.010
We think in a lot of cases
that means moving your existing

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:27.130
database software,
not re-platforming

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:28.160
onto our databases.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:31.070
It means moving your existing
choices around operating system

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.680
and file system instead of
replacing those with more

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:35.229
native cloud environments.

00:22:35.229 --> 00:22:36.020
We've got partners.

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:37.460
There's a great
booth downstairs.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:38.293
You can take a look.

00:22:38.293 --> 00:22:39.950
CloudEndure will
help you migrate a VM

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:41.074
within a click of a button.

00:22:41.074 --> 00:22:44.330
So if you've already got VMs in
VMWare or Zen or anything else,

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:46.580
those kinds of pieces move
over to us very seamlessly

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:48.480
very fast.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:51.140
The other piece, once
you've taken something

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:53.960
and you have it in
practice on top of us,

00:22:53.960 --> 00:22:56.600
you really have an
opportunity to experiment

00:22:56.600 --> 00:23:00.430
with our more modern, more
advanced tool systems.

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:05.570
So take something that's running
in MySQL, maybe 5.6 or 5.7

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:08.390
today, unplug that and
plug it into a Spanner

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:12.200
and see what the
behavior differences are.

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:15.470
Observe inside of your code,
like how much DML do you use?

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:17.366
How many inserts and
updates are there?

00:23:17.366 --> 00:23:18.740
Is that something
where Spanner's

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:20.281
going to be compatible
for your work,

00:23:20.281 --> 00:23:22.030
or is it not going to be a fit?

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:25.250
Take a look at especially
taking advantage

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:26.720
of our network infrastructure.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:29.450
So anything behind our CDNs,
behind our load balancers,

00:23:29.450 --> 00:23:32.750
behind our front-ends,
those have massive latency

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:35.570
and performance improvements,
particularly for web facing,

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:38.097
customer facing
front-end applications.

00:23:38.097 --> 00:23:39.680
We have, again, this
enormous network.

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:40.520
There's no reason
you shouldn't be

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:41.780
able to take advantage of it.

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:44.300
And you can do that even with
a single application running

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:47.120
in a single region
or a single zone.

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:50.510
You're still connected to
Google's global front-end.

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:53.810
So this is the button on
the CloudEndure piece.

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:56.240
Certainly importing virtual
machines is important.

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:59.159
I don't want to underestimate
the degree to which most

00:23:59.159 --> 00:24:01.700
of us in the operations field
are absolutely feeling pressure

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:02.480
from developers--

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:04.040
I hear it quite a lot--

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:06.890
to move from virtual
machine-based infrastructures

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:09.530
or baking images or these
other kinds of deployment

00:24:09.530 --> 00:24:12.680
modalities into
container-based systems.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:14.540
We are the ones that
built Kubernetes.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:17.000
We're the ones that built
the Linux C group that

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:19.160
backs all of this
container technology,

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:21.710
all of Google's
infrastructure everywhere,

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:23.810
every application that
you run, including

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:27.664
these virtual machines,
sits inside of a container.

00:24:27.664 --> 00:24:29.830
So we have a little experience
operating this stuff.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:32.620
It really does create a
very substantial performance

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:35.320
and efficiency benefit
for your applications.

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:38.470
If this virtual machine,
because it's gigabytes in size,

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:42.520
takes seconds to load, an
individual container inside

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:45.200
of our application architecture
takes milliseconds,

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.990
50, 55 milliseconds.

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:48.940
If you want to see
that in practice,

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:51.730
I think it's the funnest
demo of the whole thing.

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:53.500
You go out to the main
booth, and there's

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:57.460
a thing that looks like one
of those circus whack-a-mole

00:24:57.460 --> 00:24:58.830
hammer machines.

00:24:58.830 --> 00:24:59.920
You've seen this?

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:02.560
So it's got little holes in
the table and a little hammer,

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:03.670
and you smash the mole.

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:07.330
Each mole is actually a current
running container inside

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:08.960
of a Kubernetes cluster.

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.090
And it's one application.

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:12.560
It has nine copies
in the container.

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:17.220
You can't hit the moles fast
enough to bring the app down.

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:18.970
There's a little special
one in the corner

00:25:18.970 --> 00:25:20.770
so you can prove to yourself
that in fact the application

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:22.270
goes down because
it kills them all.

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:25.624
But you can't smash
and slay containers

00:25:25.624 --> 00:25:27.790
quickly enough for us to
not go, huh, now it's down.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:28.750
Huh, let's build a new one.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:29.541
Huh, let's boot it.

00:25:29.541 --> 00:25:31.230
Huh, let's launch it.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:33.760
That all happens
in milliseconds.

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:36.580
Individual keypresses
are tens of milliseconds,

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:38.290
hundreds of milliseconds.

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:41.740
So that transition operationally
for lots of businesses

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:43.870
is creating very real benefits.

00:25:43.870 --> 00:25:45.400
Not only from efficiency, right?

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.190
You can cram a lot of containers
onto individual machines.

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:50.920
You can load balance
between sets of containers.

00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:53.530
That's a pre-built
part of Kubernetes.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:57.220
But it also ensures really great
benefits from an availability

00:25:57.220 --> 00:25:57.734
standpoint.

00:25:57.734 --> 00:25:59.150
You have one virtual
machine fail,

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:01.160
and that's running all
of your application.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:02.570
The application has failed.

00:26:02.570 --> 00:26:04.510
But if you've got a
fleet of virtual machines

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:06.468
and your application is
distributed across them

00:26:06.468 --> 00:26:09.070
in clusters, it takes
milliseconds to bring back

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:12.790
online inside intra user
event kinds of signals.

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:15.420
It's very powerful stuff.

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:18.030
So we also have a very
rich and very mature

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:19.219
networking structure.

00:26:19.219 --> 00:26:20.760
I hear from a lot
of operations folks

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:23.040
that the number one
impediment to their ability

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:26.232
to adopt public cloud is
access to the kinds of network

00:26:26.232 --> 00:26:27.690
configuration and
control that they

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:30.720
need to remain compliant
with their business's best

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:31.530
practices.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:34.020
You already have
super clear run books

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:36.870
that say you will open these
ports and these protocols

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.360
to these IP ranges for
applications inside

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:41.880
of these individual VLANs.

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.604
Those building blocks
are present on us.

00:26:44.604 --> 00:26:46.020
We call them some
different things

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:47.820
because we're
maniacs about naming,

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.390
because I think all nerds
are maniacs about naming.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.800
It all started with
writing my guys

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:57.660
name is Attar of Azeroth at the
top of your character sheet.

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:59.350
We're all terrible
about that stuff.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:03.880
So our network systems--

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:06.370
the advantage that
you have is that it

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:10.420
feels like everything is
behind one giant terabit

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:11.940
a second firewall.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:14.500
You have only one place to
make these rule changes.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:18.790
You have only one system to
never have to patch again.

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:22.210
You don't have to do rolling
updates of new hardware

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:24.370
additions or rolling
redistributions of network

00:27:24.370 --> 00:27:25.090
structure.

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:26.744
All that stuff is
built in with us.

00:27:26.744 --> 00:27:28.160
We have the same
kinds of controls

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:31.210
that you need-- ingress,
egress filtration,

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:33.400
cross project networking
now is making it

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:35.470
so that you can build
isolated projects that

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:39.970
can pass individual channels
of information back and forth

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:41.680
in secured controlled ways.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.450
We have managed VPN.

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:45.850
We have the ability to build
private interconnect to us,

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:48.490
a thing called Google
Cloud Interconnect.

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:50.545
That allows you to
route private linkages,

00:27:50.545 --> 00:27:54.070
so you can take MPLS to
any colo provider that's

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.590
on our network edge, and
we're on the other side

00:27:56.590 --> 00:27:57.260
of the internet.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:58.770
So we're in most of the edges.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:02.170
At 77 of the [? ISXI pairing ?]
points in the world,

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:04.600
and that's not a
big number in total.

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:06.341
There's only about 100 of them.

00:28:06.341 --> 00:28:07.840
We're actually at
the rest of those,

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:10.660
but there aren't places where
there's leftover colo space.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:13.690
So you've got to be able to
get into those facilities.

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:17.500
You can also use
new services from us

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:19.660
that ensure that the
connectivity is private.

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:21.430
You can manage your
own keys there.

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:23.830
You have your own control
over the inscription ciphers

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:24.670
that are used.

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:28.360
You really have a very rich
platform for network control

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:31.260
to ensure that your best
practices can be reused.

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:34.000
And our hope and
our expectation is

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.520
that over time we can continue
to lower the barrier of entry

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:37.090
there.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:39.100
We want to make it so
that you can do things

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:42.040
like copying your current
network configuration

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:44.230
and profiles and
running that on us.

00:28:44.230 --> 00:28:46.870
So we have a tool
in GitHub right now

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.140
if you're running an AWS,
you can copy your firewall

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:54.340
configurations and paste them
on us and they work right.

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:55.780
No fat fingers.

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:59.650
No accidental whoopsies
on the firewall rules.

00:28:59.650 --> 00:29:01.990
That kind of simplification
where we're just

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:04.510
trying to think like
operators and think

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:06.580
about reducing the
risk of implementation,

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:08.620
reducing the
challenges associated

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:10.660
with doing this the right way.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:13.540
That's a big focus for my
team and other teams at Google

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:18.070
as we embrace these
kinds of workloads.

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:19.990
So we also really
think that there's just

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:23.020
a lot of routinized
grunt work that

00:29:23.020 --> 00:29:24.610
goes into this kind of thing.

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:26.920
A particular pain
point that I've seen

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:30.370
is being able to comply
with regulatory oversight.

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:33.520
How many folks are stoked
about their next audit?

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:34.765
Note, no hands.

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:41.310
Audit compliance is a monster,
and it's really brutal

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:43.530
if you're the one
that's manually

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:46.110
constructing all of the logs
of the changes that you made.

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:47.970
Or even if you're using
automation to do it,

00:29:47.970 --> 00:29:50.750
those become themselves
systems of record

00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:53.310
that now you must maintain
and operate, and keep track

00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:54.330
of, and make sure
they don't crash,

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:55.980
and don't lose their data,
and backup their data,

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:57.063
and recover those backups.

00:29:57.063 --> 00:30:00.440
Because you can't trust backups
if you haven't recovered them.

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:03.380
Don't do that.

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:06.410
So our systems are already
doing all of that built in.

00:30:06.410 --> 00:30:09.350
So every functional change that
you make in our infrastructure

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:10.220
is audit logged.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:12.420
We have a comprehensive
trail of that log.

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:15.080
You're able to produce
copies of that log on demand.

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:18.934
They are stored in GCS storage,
which does not lose objects.

00:30:18.934 --> 00:30:21.350
So you don't have to do the
backup work or the maintenance

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:22.040
work.

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:23.164
You need that stuff shared.

00:30:23.164 --> 00:30:24.890
That's all available
online right now.

00:30:24.890 --> 00:30:27.440
And it's impossible for
one of your employees

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:29.030
or one of the folks
that you manage

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:31.500
to be able to launch something
that doesn't get logged.

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:34.400
How many folks have
had like that one guy

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:37.649
stuff that one extra laptop
in the corner of the back rack

00:30:37.649 --> 00:30:39.440
that makes it so
everything works, but it's

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:44.170
on nobody's spreadsheets, and
it's got no anti-virus on it?

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:46.880
This stuff goes
terrible real fast.

00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:50.000
In cloud, you can't get in.

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:51.770
There's no backdoor.

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:53.240
There's no extra
spot where I can

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:56.960
stuff the weird toys that I want
to play with on the weekend.

00:30:56.960 --> 00:30:58.460
So we've really
made it so that this

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:01.970
is a place where you can exert
a little more control, a little

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:04.010
more oversight and
management to ensure

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:06.980
that your constituency is the
folks that you're managing

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:10.070
for are following the rules.

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:13.010
And for us what we've
seen is that that

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:15.410
makes the biggest impact
in these kinds of security

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:16.940
and compliance pieces.

00:31:16.940 --> 00:31:20.360
A big part is just offloading
all of the operations

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:23.780
work associated with achieving
compliance for everything

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:26.630
below the virtual machine.

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:29.930
We're the ones who have to go
through and prove to the PCI

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:33.770
DSS folks that, yep, these
computers are in point of fact

00:31:33.770 --> 00:31:36.410
safe to process credit
card transactions on.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:38.870
And then all you have to
do is the audit controls

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:40.640
of your application above that.

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:44.320
That's just a much smaller
surface area of management.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:48.530
And if all of a sudden, like,
say today, you work in retail,

00:31:48.530 --> 00:31:50.210
and so you're
processing transactions.

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:52.168
But then all of a sudden
one of your sales guys

00:31:52.168 --> 00:31:55.010
gets like super smart and starts
to sell a part of your product

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:56.330
into health care--

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:57.200
oops.

00:31:57.200 --> 00:31:59.720
You have to be HIPPA
compliant all of a sudden.

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:00.740
Error.

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:03.890
If you built a compliance
regime and a modeling

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:06.560
and a records system for
your operational tooling

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:08.955
that's designed around one
compliance system, and then

00:32:08.955 --> 00:32:10.580
all of a sudden you
have to accommodate

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:14.250
another, that can be a huge
retooling and rebuild exercise.

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:16.760
I've helped companies do that.

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:18.740
So with public
cloud you're already

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:22.640
achieving all of the
standards that we've already

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:23.930
signed up for.

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:28.482
So you already are PCI
DSS level 3 certified

00:32:28.482 --> 00:32:29.690
for the infrastructure stack.

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:32.360
We've done that now with
dozens, hundreds of businesses.

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:34.012
You can check that
off your list.

00:32:34.012 --> 00:32:35.720
You don't ever have
to work on that part.

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:37.966
And the auditors-- they know.

00:32:37.966 --> 00:32:39.590
They've gone to other
customers of ours

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:42.031
and they go, oh, this
is the Google Cloud one?

00:32:42.031 --> 00:32:42.530
OK.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:42.980
Marvelous.

00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:43.880
Yeah, don't worry about it.

00:32:43.880 --> 00:32:45.650
We know that they have
that part handled.

00:32:45.650 --> 00:32:49.310
You don't have to re-explain
it the 800th time.

00:32:49.310 --> 00:32:52.557
So that can be very powerful
and can be a very big impact.

00:32:52.557 --> 00:32:53.890
So I said a bunch of good news--

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:55.060
public cloud sounds super great.

00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:55.690
Right?

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:56.340
You're all going to move.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:57.381
It's going to be awesome.

00:32:57.381 --> 00:32:59.000
Everything is going
to be awesome.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:02.800
The reality is that
it's not all done.

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:07.030
Right now the ratio to sales
from salespeople to engineers

00:33:07.030 --> 00:33:08.890
is deeply biased on
the engineering side.

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:12.730
So there is clearly way
more for us left to build.

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:15.640
So what are the things that
are still hard or complex?

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:18.190
What places can you
anticipate that you're

00:33:18.190 --> 00:33:20.800
going to have to do a little
extra operations work in order

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:24.734
to be able to accommodate the
challenges of this system?

00:33:24.734 --> 00:33:26.650
First off is-- remember
how I said that it was

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:28.560
like super easy to build stuff?

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:30.545
It's super easy to build stuff.

00:33:30.545 --> 00:33:32.920
Which means that you're going
to build all sorts of stuff

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:35.110
like super fast, and
it's going to go out

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:36.640
into the reaches
of the inter web.

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:38.832
There will be copies
of your software

00:33:38.832 --> 00:33:41.290
that your engineers decided
they wanted one more build box,

00:33:41.290 --> 00:33:42.960
but they wanted it in Singapore.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:45.190
And they want it in
the middle of nowhere.

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:48.970
That can create very
substantial sprawl.

00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:50.920
It is easy to delete stuff.

00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:52.200
I made that clear.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:54.940
But I think many
businesses because they're

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:59.200
used to deleting, meaning
de-racking and shoving

00:33:59.200 --> 00:34:03.090
in a chipper shredder, that
all of a sudden deletions

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:07.210
are to be carefully done.

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:10.110
If I delete this, maybe
somebody else's thing turns off.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:12.830
And I don't know where the
sort of isolation layers are,

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:14.860
and maybe I'll just
leave that thing there.

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:17.770
So I certainly
encourage that the more

00:34:17.770 --> 00:34:20.380
work you do in programmatic
management, the more work

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:23.530
that you do in automated
deployment development,

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:26.440
that you match that
effort with the effort

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:29.679
to track and use our--

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:31.840
very clear you have great
tagging capabilities,

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:34.210
great reporting and auditing
capability on our side.

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:35.959
You can certainly keep
track of this stuff

00:34:35.959 --> 00:34:37.040
and be able to manage it.

00:34:37.040 --> 00:34:38.530
But it is an
absolute place where

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:41.500
we see customers have trouble.

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:44.560
It's also super easy to
use these tools when you

00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:46.489
have three virtual machines.

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:48.120
You name the first one web.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:50.040
You name the second one app.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:52.290
You name the third one DB.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:54.340
And it's right
there on a screen.

00:34:54.340 --> 00:34:56.360
Yeah, my stack is sweet.

00:34:56.360 --> 00:35:00.150
And then you have 750 VMs.

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:01.292
It shows the first 10.

00:35:01.292 --> 00:35:03.250
And then at the bottom
it has a little box that

00:35:03.250 --> 00:35:07.170
says this is page one of 75.

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:10.510
And you go that's
going to be somewhat

00:35:10.510 --> 00:35:14.000
daunting to find my little
snazzy web and app and DB.

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:20.070
So these interfaces are
absolutely designed and built

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:22.920
around the experience
of our developers

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:26.130
and our users who are
building a project for them.

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:28.390
They have to be easy to adopt.

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:33.220
They have to be able to use
and easy to sort of build

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:34.910
your own first thing.

00:35:34.910 --> 00:35:36.550
And so there are
absolutely places

00:35:36.550 --> 00:35:40.750
where rigor around naming,
rigor around structure,

00:35:40.750 --> 00:35:42.760
will save you from
interfaces that

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:44.500
are just utterly unassailable.

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:46.360
The other piece is
once you've gotten

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:50.350
to a place where your
application is now really

00:35:50.350 --> 00:35:54.380
more than one copy of
an individual layer.

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:57.820
So I have seven web machines,
and I have 18 app machines,

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:01.240
and I got a brazillion
DBM machines.

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:04.330
That summarization is
something that's still not

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:06.152
particularly good on our tools.

00:36:06.152 --> 00:36:08.110
Dashboards don't really
summarize that stuff up

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:08.942
very well.

00:36:08.942 --> 00:36:11.150
User interfaces don't
summarize that stuff very well.

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:14.050
So you may end up
in a situation where

00:36:14.050 --> 00:36:17.062
you end up having to
supplement our views--

00:36:17.062 --> 00:36:18.520
building some of
your own reporting

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:20.170
from your own
analysis that reflects

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:21.295
that kind of summarization.

00:36:21.295 --> 00:36:23.169
I think we'll continue
to work on that stuff.

00:36:23.169 --> 00:36:25.147
I think that's a
challenge for any system.

00:36:25.147 --> 00:36:27.730
Does the picture look good when
it's right up against my face?

00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:30.437
And does it look good when
I'm zoomed out 1,000 times?

00:36:30.437 --> 00:36:32.020
That's the kind of
challenge we're in.

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:35.720
But it's certainly a place
to keep paying attention to.

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:41.410
We also have in our environment,
and you can certainly configure

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:45.880
machines in a way in your
own networks that are widely

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:46.960
spec'd.

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:51.220
So you could have the
worst conceivable case.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:54.230
You could have a
four socket box.

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:57.090
But then you buy the
cheapest dirtiest xeons

00:36:57.090 --> 00:36:59.470
that there are with two
cores a piece or something.

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:03.190
But then you can slap in two
terabytes or something of RAM.

00:37:03.190 --> 00:37:04.990
Or you can have these
unbelievable ram

00:37:04.990 --> 00:37:06.370
to compute ratios.

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:09.280
We today leave those
relatively narrow--

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:12.550
6 and 1/2 to 1, to 1/5 to 1--

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:14.410
or 1/2 to 1, sorry.

00:37:14.410 --> 00:37:18.970
So the narrowness of that,
if you have very extreme ram

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:21.160
to compute ratios,
those can make it

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:23.350
so that you end up
over provisioning

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:24.610
the processing that you need.

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:28.180
In the same way, our network
is bound to the compute

00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:29.232
that you consume.

00:37:29.232 --> 00:37:30.940
So you may be in an
environment, like say

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:33.310
you're trying to run
Redis or memcached,

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:36.070
where you're really looking at I
want a box with maximum memory,

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:37.480
maximum network.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.940
You might have to bring
a little extra CPU along

00:37:39.940 --> 00:37:42.790
on the ride to get our systems
to give you the network

00:37:42.790 --> 00:37:44.050
throughput that you need.

00:37:44.050 --> 00:37:47.140
Now, we really over
provision network.

00:37:47.140 --> 00:37:51.070
We give you two gigabits
a second per core.

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:53.380
So you're going to have
an 8 core VM with 16

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:55.030
gig a second networking.

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:59.270
And that's not like 16 gigs of
theoretical and then real world

00:37:59.270 --> 00:38:01.000
you get like 11
and 1/2, and you're

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:03.470
in there sort of trying to do
MTU reconfiguration to figure

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:05.290
it out-- no, it's 16.

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:06.910
You can do the
test all day long.

00:38:06.910 --> 00:38:07.670
They work great.

00:38:07.670 --> 00:38:09.420
And you can have a
cluster of 100 of them.

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:10.600
They'll all do 16.

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:11.980
It's very, very fast networking.

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:14.980
But if you need
quick networking,

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:17.320
you're going to end up using
at least that eight core

00:38:17.320 --> 00:38:19.150
line to get there.

00:38:19.150 --> 00:38:20.650
And that's an important measure.

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:24.190
That becomes interesting
as we continue

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:27.400
to add more raw materials
to the virtual machines.

00:38:27.400 --> 00:38:31.000
So today you have what
we call custom VMs.

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:33.490
You can adjust how many
cores and how much memory

00:38:33.490 --> 00:38:35.270
you need per VM.

00:38:35.270 --> 00:38:37.294
You want a 16 core.

00:38:37.294 --> 00:38:37.960
No, forget that.

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:42.241
You want an 18 core VM, and
you want 46.25 gigs of RAM.

00:38:42.241 --> 00:38:42.740
Awesome.

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:43.990
We will sell you one of those.

00:38:43.990 --> 00:38:45.820
We'll charge you for
only what you need.

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:47.860
But you may also
want SSD on there,

00:38:47.860 --> 00:38:50.240
and you may also
want GPU on there.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:52.942
We'll enable-- and it's
totally unique to Google Cloud.

00:38:52.942 --> 00:38:55.150
It's one of the things that
I think is most powerful.

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:57.610
We enable you to
individually assign--

00:38:57.610 --> 00:39:02.680
I want three 375 gig
slices of SSD on this VM

00:39:02.680 --> 00:39:05.170
with 46.25 gigs of RAM.

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:11.470
And I want four slots
of K80 and Nvidia GPU.

00:39:11.470 --> 00:39:14.140
That's a monster virtual
machine we just built up.

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:18.040
But because there are sort of
limits in that scalability,

00:39:18.040 --> 00:39:20.950
you have to make sure that
the ratios that you're using

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:23.069
fit into what we document
as a best practice.

00:39:23.069 --> 00:39:23.860
So that's critical.

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:28.900
This last one is funny-- yes.

00:39:28.900 --> 00:39:31.520
You will certainly end up
in relation to the first one

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:33.710
turning things off and
having people squeal.

00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:37.220
I can also sort of say that
some of the important places

00:39:37.220 --> 00:39:39.890
to pay attention
to beyond this--

00:39:39.890 --> 00:39:42.980
we take very
seriously, many of you

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:45.110
may be because you're
here at the conference,

00:39:45.110 --> 00:39:47.480
your early customers,
your aggressive customers.

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:50.690
Google follows a very
clear sequenced step

00:39:50.690 --> 00:39:52.220
of product releases.

00:39:52.220 --> 00:39:55.370
So we do a thing called early
access that gives people access

00:39:55.370 --> 00:39:58.334
to applications before
we think they're done.

00:39:58.334 --> 00:40:00.500
And that means that they
can crash and burn and die.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:03.200
And we change them, and they do
all sorts of terrible things.

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:07.760
We have alpha products that
we think of as probably done,

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:11.750
but we're way beyond
being able to set an SLA

00:40:11.750 --> 00:40:14.810
or even understand probably what
pricing will look like or be

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:17.000
able to assert any kind of SLA.

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:19.430
So alpha products some of
our customers get access to.

00:40:19.430 --> 00:40:22.130
Lots of our customers get
access to beta products.

00:40:22.130 --> 00:40:24.830
Those we also typically keep
the price points off of,

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:27.020
and we're not willing
to sign up for an SLA.

00:40:27.020 --> 00:40:28.910
But we think they're
in a final state.

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:33.200
We think that your code won't
change when it moves to GA.

00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:36.480
We think that we've got
the API code complete.

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:38.870
So most of our customers,
most of our developers,

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:41.470
feel very comfortable
with beta software.

00:40:41.470 --> 00:40:44.630
An important sort of
threshold in that, a way

00:40:44.630 --> 00:40:48.020
to anticipate the
degree of confidence

00:40:48.020 --> 00:40:51.460
that you can have in
beta to GA software,

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:54.200
is to look and see if that's
a product that's been running

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:56.300
in Google for a long time.

00:40:56.300 --> 00:41:00.690
So we've put Cloud
Spanner into beta.

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:03.774
Now, Cloud Spanner powers every
one of the systems at Google.

00:41:03.774 --> 00:41:06.440
So you have to imagine that it's
not like we're building Spanner

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:07.360
over again.

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:09.560
What we're doing is we're
adding an API layer that

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:11.900
allows us to charge you for it.

00:41:11.900 --> 00:41:14.164
And I suspect that the
operations around the API layer

00:41:14.164 --> 00:41:15.830
to charge you for it
are nowhere near as

00:41:15.830 --> 00:41:19.580
complex as the cesium clocks
in the GPS devices that

00:41:19.580 --> 00:41:21.470
are on the racks and
all the crazy software

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:24.170
that it takes to
make Spanner work.

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.200
So in our environments, we're
seeing lots of customers

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:30.836
build production systems
on beta services.

00:41:30.836 --> 00:41:32.210
That may be
different than you're

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:33.900
used to in other environments.

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:36.110
And so as you're going
through, certainly

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:38.074
interact with your
customer engineer,

00:41:38.074 --> 00:41:39.740
get some of the context
from your peers,

00:41:39.740 --> 00:41:41.156
reach out to the
broader community

00:41:41.156 --> 00:41:43.790
to understand what they're
seeing in performance

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:45.770
and availability for
the services that

00:41:45.770 --> 00:41:48.680
are in alpha and certainly
in beta as you move forward.

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:51.980
We are also very aggressive
about moving to GA.

00:41:51.980 --> 00:41:53.377
We take GA very seriously.

00:41:53.377 --> 00:41:54.710
It's a very different threshold.

00:41:54.710 --> 00:41:58.310
We absolutely commit to SLAs
on all products that are in GA.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:42:01.909
And so you'll see the top level
of Google's support and service

00:42:01.909 --> 00:42:02.700
for those launches.

00:42:02.700 --> 00:42:06.530
That's why it's so exciting
to hear that the ML engine

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:09.470
product has now gone GA,
because you can really

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:11.450
start to look at
businesses getting

00:42:11.450 --> 00:42:13.910
ready to deploy very, very
massive machine learning

00:42:13.910 --> 00:42:17.540
systems on us and to be able
to count on a very clear SLA.

00:42:17.540 --> 00:42:20.810
It's also important for folks
that are building environments

00:42:20.810 --> 00:42:24.360
designed for availability--

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:26.820
not every SLA is the same.

00:42:26.820 --> 00:42:28.590
You should really
look at what happens

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:30.186
when those SLAs get breached.

00:42:30.186 --> 00:42:31.560
Same way as you
should always try

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:35.220
to restore your backups
from time to time.

00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:39.300
If the penalties associated
with us failing an SLA,

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:43.020
or with our competitors failing
their SLAs, aren't toothy,

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:45.245
aren't violent--

00:42:45.245 --> 00:42:46.620
I want to make
sure that you feel

00:42:46.620 --> 00:42:50.670
like your needs are managed
and your needs are satisfied.

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:52.380
Having a contractual
relationship

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:56.580
with a provider that really
binds us to shared fate,

00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:59.280
to shared responsibility
over ensuring

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:02.040
that you're successful, I
think that's a critical part.

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:03.940
We've turned that from
a contractual thing,

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:05.880
and now we're actually
doing it in practice.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:08.580
So one of my sister teams,
Customer Reliability

00:43:08.580 --> 00:43:10.500
Engineering, they'll come out.

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:13.260
And our practice with
you is once you've

00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:16.020
gone through what we call
a product reliability

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:18.540
review, a PRR, we actually
go through your application

00:43:18.540 --> 00:43:20.420
and say, nope,
that's super lame.

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:21.670
You shouldn't run it that way.

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:23.430
And that's super broken.

00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:24.780
You shouldn't do that.

00:43:24.780 --> 00:43:27.210
That's going to be difficult
for you to maintain.

00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:31.260
If you pass the same checklist
that Google applications pass,

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:34.500
then we will share
pagers with you.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:38.392
We'll take an SLA
for your app, which

00:43:38.392 --> 00:43:39.600
is a pretty incredible thing.

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:41.474
There are a couple of
presentations about CRE

00:43:41.474 --> 00:43:43.440
I certainly recommend
for the operations

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:45.090
folks to take a
look at that stuff,

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:48.030
because it's really a totally
different level of commitment

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.910
from a cloud provider to
folks running applications

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:52.060
on top of us.

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:55.089
So a simple closing haiku--

00:43:55.089 --> 00:43:56.880
I think you can probably
move to the cloud,

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:58.110
it's going to be
easier than you think,

00:43:58.110 --> 00:43:59.360
it's going to be super groovy.

00:43:59.360 --> 00:43:59.870
Thanks.

00:43:59.870 --> 00:44:03.520
[APPLAUSE]

